Stefins are a family of proteins that act as irreversible inhibitors of cysteine peptidases, including the cathepsin family. They play a role in regulating the activity of these enzymes by binding tightly to their active sites, preventing them from cleaving their substrates. This inhibition helps maintain proteolytic balance in the body. Stefins are also involved in processes such as neurite extension and cell migration, highlighting their broader regulatory functions beyond enzyme inhibition.  

\boxed{
\text{Stefins irreversibly inhibit cysteine peptidases by binding to their active sites, preventing substrate cleavage.}
}

