Isotopologues are molecules that differ in the isotopic composition of atoms, specifically in the number of additional neutrons in the nucleus. For example, \(^{12}\text{C}\)- and \(^{13}\text{C}\)-labelled versions of the same molecule are isotopologues. This distinction is preserved in their chemical structure, as the number of atoms and their connectivity remain the same.  

Isotopomers are molecules that are the same in terms of their isotopic composition but differ in the distribution of atoms. For instance, \(^{13}\text{C}\text{D}\text{onViewCreated}\) is an isotopomer of \(^{13}\text{C}\text{D}_2\text{OH}\), as they share the same isotopic composition but differ in the connectivity of the atoms.  

Thus, while allisotopomers are isotopologues, not allisopologues are isotopomers.  

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\text{All isotopomers are isotopologues, but not all isotopologues are isotopomers.} \\
&\text{Isopologues differ in isotopic composition only, while isotopomers differ in atom connectivity.}
\end{aligned}
}

To estimate the flammability limits of mixtures from the data for individual fuels, the **batch method** is used. The model is based on the concept that the mixture behaves as a linear mixture of its components. The flammable limits of the mixture are estimated as the weighted average of the flammable limits of the individual fuels, weighted by their respective amounts in the mixture.

From the model, the flammaity limits of a mixture are calculated as:
- **Lower Flammability Limit (LFL):**  
  \[
  LFL_{\text{mixture}} = \sum_{i=1}^{N} w_i \cdot LFL_{i}
  \]
  where \(w_i\) is the mass fraction of fuel \(i\) in the mixture and \(LFL_{i}\) is the LFL of fuel \(i\).

- **Upper Flammability Limit (UCL):**  
  \[
  UCL_{\text{mixture}} = \sum_{i=1}^{N} w_i \cdot UCL_{i}
  \]
  where \(UCL_{i}\) is the UCL of fuel \(i\).

This approach is widely used for mixtures where the fuels are miscible, do not react to form new compounds, and have negligible deviations from ideal behavior. However, the model may not be universally applicable due to factors like chemical interactions or non-ideal behavior. For more accurate predictions, the mixture should be tested experimentally.  

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\text{LFL}_{\text{mixture}} = \sum_{i=1}^{N} w_i \cdot \text{LFL}_{i}, \\
&\text{UCL}_{\text{mixture}} = \sum_{i=1}^{N} w_i \cdot \text{UCL}_{i}.
\end{aligned}
}

